1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,768
Chai Corner has six people now,
and Ravi dreams of more outlets.

2
00:00:05,068 --> 00:00:08,388
But when Ravi steps out, everyone
does things their own way.

3
00:00:08,938 --> 00:00:11,485
One helper gives free chai to his friends.

4
00:00:11,785 --> 00:00:14,077
Another forgets to write down the sales.

5
00:00:14,377 --> 00:00:17,958
And the best shift goes to Ravi's
cousin, not the best worker.

6
00:00:18,608 --> 00:00:20,781
More outlets won't need more Ravi.

7
00:00:20,781 --> 00:00:22,262
They need a rulebook.

8
00:00:22,962 --> 00:00:26,901
Over a hundred years ago, a
German thinker had the same idea.

9
00:00:27,201 --> 00:00:30,314
His name was Max Weber.

10
00:00:30,314 --> 00:00:33,904
He lived from 1864 to 1920.

11
00:00:34,254 --> 00:00:37,913
He was a sociologist, someone
who studies people in groups.

12
00:00:38,263 --> 00:00:40,460
He called his idea bureaucracy.

13
00:00:40,760 --> 00:00:44,349
It means clear written rules,
and a clear chain of command.

14
00:00:44,999 --> 00:00:46,908
Weber listed seven features.

15
00:00:46,908 --> 00:00:49,755
Let's write them into
Chai Corner's rulebook.

16
00:00:50,205 --> 00:00:51,105
One.

17
00:00:50,766 --> 00:00:52,282
Division of labour.

18
00:00:52,282 --> 00:00:54,601
Each person has one clear job.

19
00:00:54,951 --> 00:00:55,851
Two.

20
00:00:55,495 --> 00:00:59,112
A hierarchy of authority,
like a ladder of bosses.

21
00:00:59,462 --> 00:01:00,362
Three.

22
00:01:00,117 --> 00:01:01,705
Formal written rules.

23
00:01:01,705 --> 00:01:04,183
Every cup follows the same recipe.

24
00:01:04,533 --> 00:01:05,433
Four.

25
00:01:05,158 --> 00:01:06,285
Impersonality.

26
00:01:06,285 --> 00:01:08,975
The rules apply equally to everyone.

27
00:01:08,975 --> 00:01:10,984
No free chai for friends.

28
00:01:11,334 --> 00:01:12,234
Five.

29
00:01:11,922 --> 00:01:12,822
Merit.

30
00:01:12,562 --> 00:01:16,548
People are hired and promoted
for skill, not family ties.

31
00:01:16,898 --> 00:01:17,798
Six.

32
00:01:17,386 --> 00:01:18,286
Career.

33
00:01:18,019 --> 00:01:21,528
Staff earn a fixed salary,
and can grow step by step.

34
00:01:21,877 --> 00:01:22,787
And seven.

35
00:01:22,787 --> 00:01:24,009
Written records.

36
00:01:24,009 --> 00:01:26,800
Every sale and decision is written down.

37
00:01:27,450 --> 00:01:29,358
Why do people obey a boss?

38
00:01:29,358 --> 00:01:31,815
Weber found three kinds of authority.

39
00:01:32,165 --> 00:01:33,094
Traditional.

40
00:01:33,094 --> 00:01:35,778
We obey because it has always been so.

41
00:01:36,128 --> 00:01:37,105
Charismatic.

42
00:01:37,105 --> 00:01:39,209
We obey a leader we admire.

43
00:01:39,559 --> 00:01:40,852
And rational-legal.

44
00:01:40,852 --> 00:01:43,368
We obey the rules, not the person.

45
00:01:43,368 --> 00:01:45,405
Bureaucracy is built on this.

46
00:01:46,055 --> 00:01:47,761
So, is bureaucracy good?

47
00:01:47,761 --> 00:01:50,297
It is fair, orderly, and predictable.

48
00:01:50,647 --> 00:01:54,639
But too much paperwork, called
red tape, makes it rigid and slow.

49
00:01:54,989 --> 00:01:59,374
A customer asks for less sugar, and a
strict helper says: not in the rules.

50
00:01:59,724 --> 00:02:03,739
So Ravi keeps the rulebook short,
and trusts his team's common sense.

51
00:02:04,389 --> 00:02:05,349
Quick check.

52
00:02:05,349 --> 00:02:09,357
Kabir's friend pays the usual
price, like every other customer.

53
00:02:09,357 --> 00:02:10,931
Which feature is this?

54
00:02:12,831 --> 00:02:13,847
Impersonality!

55
00:02:13,847 --> 00:02:17,458
The rules apply equally to
everyone, whoever they are.

56
00:02:18,058 --> 00:02:19,067
So, remember.

57
00:02:19,367 --> 00:02:23,288
Bureaucracy means clear rules,
and a clear chain of command.

58
00:02:23,638 --> 00:02:28,232
Its features include division of
labour, hierarchy, rules, impersonality,

59
00:02:28,232 --> 00:02:29,132
and merit.

60
00:02:29,444 --> 00:02:31,224
It brings fairness and order.

61
00:02:31,224 --> 00:02:33,477
But too much of it becomes red tape.

62
00:02:34,077 --> 00:02:38,504
Next time, we'll meet Elton Mayo, and
see why people work better when they

63
00:02:38,504 --> 00:02:39,410
feel valued.
